.\" original page (this project's own text), so it carries no
.\" third-party notice -- MIT like the code, same posture as
.\" cksum.1/paste.1. Rendered at build time to build/man/CSPLIT.TXT
.\" and staged on the play image as /usr/man/CSPLIT.
.TH CSPLIT 1 "September 15, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
csplit \- split a file into pieces at context lines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B csplit
[
.B \-k
] [
.B \-s
] [
.B \-f
.I prefix
] [
.B \-n
.I number
]
.I file
.I arg
.RI [ arg
.IR ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Csplit
reads
.I file
and writes it out in pieces at the boundaries named by each
.IR arg ,
processed in order, current position starting at line 1:
.TP
.I number
A boundary right before that (1-based) line.
.I number
equal to the current line makes an empty piece;
.I number
less than it is an error.
.TP
.BI / bre / [+\-offset]
Search forward for the next line matching
.IR bre ;
the boundary falls
.I offset
lines (default 0) past it. The matched-through span becomes a
real piece file.
.TP
.BI % bre % [+\-offset]
The same search, but the span is discarded \(em no piece file is
created for it.
.TP
.BI { n }
Repeat the preceding
.I bre
argument
.I n
more times, each a fresh forward search from wherever the last one
left off.
.RB ( "{*}"
is a KNOWN GAP: the repeat count must be a decimal number.)
.PP
Whatever remains after the last
.I arg
becomes one final piece. Pieces are named
.I prefix
.RB ( \-f ,
default
.BR xx )
followed by a zero-padded decimal counter
.RB ( \-n
digits, default 2):
.BR xx00 ,
.BR xx01 ,
and so on. Each piece's byte count is written to standard output,
one line, right after it closes, unless
.BR \-s .
.PP
A
.I bre
that never matches before end of file, or a
.I number
not strictly greater than the current line, are errors: without
.BR \-k ,
every piece this invocation created (the partial one included) is
destroyed and the run exits 1; with
.BR \-k ,
they stay.
.I Offset
is honored only when positive (KNOWN LIMITATION: a negative offset
needs a multi-line lookback buffer this build does not carry, so it
is accepted syntactically and treated as 0). The delimiter
character's first later occurrence ends the pattern; there is no
.RI \e / \e %
escape.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/CSPLIT  this page
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on success; 1 if the input file could not be
opened, the prefix/digit-width exceeds 15 characters, a piece
could not be created or written, or an
.I arg
failed to match/order as described above; 2 on a usage error.
.SH BUGS
None known.
